d['ch2']['essay']+="\n\nI'll add one thread: the cold Ishmael feels here, in the doorways of New Bedford, is the cold the book will keep returning to as the condition of a man alone — and the cure for it is already two chapters away, in [[ch3:never slept better|the Spouter-Inn's one bed]]."
d['ch4']['essay']+="\n\nThe arm thrown over Ishmael here is worth remembering when the two of them are tied together by an actual rope in [[ch72:free will had received a mortal wound|The Monkey-Rope]] — the book keeps re-binding these two, first in affection and then in danger."
d['ch13']['essay']+="\n\nThe phrase 'a mutual, joint-stock world' is Ishmael's translation of the halved money in [[ch10:two equal portions|A Bosom Friend]] — and it will be quietly contradicted by the actual joint-stock company that owns the Pequod, whose first act in [[ch16:seven hundred and seventy-seventh|The Ship]] is to price him."
d['ch8']['essay']+="\n\nThe other pulpit in the book is Ahab's quarter-deck in [[ch36|The Quarter-Deck]], and the two are worth comparing: Mapple pulls the ladder up behind him and speaks alone; Ahab nails a coin to the mast and makes the congregation drink."
